<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Khunan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1221">1221</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Khunan · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasions-of-georgia">Mongol invasions of Georgia</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Khunan was fought in 1221 in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, where the Mongol vanguard led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> defeated the Georgian army of King George IV. It was the first Mongol attack on Georgia, part of a long reconnaissance raid around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. The unexpected defeat broke the kingdom's military confidence, and it was followed by Khwarazmian and later full Mongol invasions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-khunan.jpg" alt="Battle of Khunan" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nika Khoperia (translation by Ercwlff) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_invasion_of_Georgia-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>About 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> had pursued the ousted Khwarazmian ruler, Shah Muhammad II, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a> and then, with the consent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, gone west on a reconnaissance mission. The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> was then at its height, dominating most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. The Mongols drove Atabeg Uzbek of the Eldiguzids from Ganja to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, recruited Turkish and Kurdish forces under Akush, a disloyal underling of Uzbek, and received reinforcements from Genghis Khan.</p><p>According to Kirakos Gandzaketsi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> made their base in a very safe place between the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barda">Barda</a> and Beylagan, from which they launched attacks. The governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, Shams Tabrizi, paid a fortune to buy the city's exemption; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> slaughtered the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and then turned north, depopulating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> and Ganja, before going on into eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>King George IV and his atabeg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivane-mkhargrdzeli">Ivane Mkhargrdzeli</a> assembled about 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, and the two armies met on the plain of Khunan in September. Jebe set an ambush with 5,000 cavalrymen while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> advanced with the rest. The Mongol tactic was to attack with the main body and feign a retreat, after which a second force would descend from the rear to encircle the enemy.</p><p>Unprepared for this, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> dispersed the first Mongol charge with their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> and chased it as far as the river Kotman, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a>'s sudden advance from the ambush decided the battle. The king and Ivane fled, leaving Prince Vahram Gageli to fight on the right flank; he survived and later served under Queen Rusudan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>George IV was badly wounded in the chest and died of his injuries on 18 January 1223. Further Mongol invasions were called off when scouts found that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were prepared to resist new incursions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> instead marched north with orders to conquer the Polovtsian Khanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Khunan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This strength proved brittle: the Mongol vanguard of Jebe and Subutai defeated George IV at <b>Khunan</b> in 1221, and the Khwarazmian ruler Jalal al-Din sacked Tbilisi in 1226.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
